{
  "info": {
    "_postman_id": "b7c1e2a4-5f3d-4a91-9c88-relay000001",
    "name": "Relay",
    "description": "End-to-end collection for the Relay messenger.\n\n## Before you run\n\nStart infrastructure and services:\n\n```\ndocker compose up -d                     # keycloak, kafka, postgres x4, redis\n(cd eureka && ./gradlew bootRun)         # :8761\n(cd api-gateway && ./gradlew bootRun)    # :8080\n(cd auth && ./gradlew bootRun)           # random port, reached via the gateway\n(cd user && ./gradlew bootRun --args='--server.port=8085')\n(cd message && ./gradlew bootRun --args='--server.port=8084')\n(cd websocket-gateway && ./gradlew bootRun)  # :8083\n```\n\n`user` and `message` ship with `server.port: 0`, so they get a random port and are\nnormally reached only through Eureka. This collection calls their `/internal` endpoints\ndirectly, which needs a fixed port — hence the `--server.port` overrides above. Change the\n`userServiceUrl` / `messageServiceUrl` variables if you prefer different ones.\n\nKeycloak must have the `relay-realm` realm, a `relay-client` client with the secret in\n`auth/src/main/resources/application.yaml`, and a client role named `USER`.\n\n## How to run\n\nUse **Run collection** — the folders are ordered and later requests depend on variables\ncaptured by earlier ones (tokens, user ids, dialog ids). Running requests one by one works\ntoo, as long as you go top to bottom.\n\n## WebSocket\n\nPostman collections cannot carry WebSocket requests, so the socket is not in here. All frames\nuse the versioned envelope of ARCHITECTURE.md §10: `{\"v\":1,\"type\":\"<dot.name>\",\"id\":\"<uuid>\",\n\"ts\":<epoch-ms>,\"payload\":{...}}` with snake_case payload keys. The envelope `id` of a\n`message.send` IS the client message id (idempotency + ack correlation).\n\n**1. Postman native WebSocket request** — New > WebSocket, URL `ws://localhost:8083/ws`.\nUnder *Protocols* add two entries: `access_token` and then your access token value. Connect,\nthen send:\n\n```json\n{\"v\":1,\"type\":\"ping\",\"id\":\"p-1\"}\n{\"v\":1,\"type\":\"message.send\",\"id\":\"<uuid>\",\"ts\":1730000000000,\"payload\":{\"dialog_id\":\"<dialogId>\",\"text\":\"hello\"}}\n```\n\n**2. Browser console** on any page:\n\n```js\nconst jwt = '<paste accessTokenA>';\nconst ws = new WebSocket('ws://localhost:8083/ws', ['access_token', jwt]);\nws.onmessage = e => console.log(e.data);\nws.onopen = () => ws.send(JSON.stringify({v:1, type:'ping', id:'p-1'}));\n// then:\n// ws.send(JSON.stringify({v:1, type:'message.send', id:crypto.randomUUID(), ts:Date.now(),\n//                         payload:{dialog_id:'<dialogId>', text:'hi'}}))\n```\n\nThe token rides in the subprotocol list rather than the URL, so it stays out of access logs and\nbrowser history. Expect `session.connected` first; a send is answered with `ack` (correlated by\n`client_msg_id` = your envelope id) once the message is persisted — the send travels\ngateway → Kafka `messages.incoming` → message-service → Postgres → Kafka `messages.delivery` →\ngateway. Recipients (including your own other devices) get `message.new`. Failures come back as\n`error` frames whose `ref_id` echoes your envelope id — that is the cue to retry the same id\nover the REST fallback. To watch delivery between two users, open a second socket as user B and\nsend as user A.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
